  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nHeat remains a concern through the first half of the week with above \nnormal temperatures continuing under a persistent ridge of high \npressure. The high pressure will also inhibit convective \ndevelopment, although a rogue shower or thunderstorm along the sea \nbreeze, can not be ruled out. Most locations will remain dry.\n\nIt is looking like we will see some relief from the heat and lack of \nrain beginning mid-week. The ridge is forecast to shift east as a \nmid/upper level long wave trough digs across the western U.S. \nSufficient moisture combined with embedded short waves ahead of the \nmain long wave trough, will be conducive for convective development \nbeginning Tuesday with a 20-35% chance across the eastern CWA.\n\nModels indicate that the long wave trough will deepen and become cut \noff as it approaches West Texas. This will strengthen the low level \nwinds across S TX and usher deeper moisture across the area from the \ngulf in the low and mid levels. The cut-off low will also tap into \nmid/upper level moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Polo in the \nPacific. \n\nA cold front associated with the mid/upper low is progged to move \ninto N TX on Wednesday while a pre-frontal surface trough develops \nacross S TX. This surface trough will provide low level moisture \nconvergence and increase rain chances to 30-50% across the Victoria \nCrossroads on Wednesday. Models indicate widespread showers and \nthunderstorms by Thursday as the upper level moisture from the \nPacific arrives and PWATs increase to 2.2-2.5 inches. This is almost \n2 standard deviations above normal and is conducive for heavy \nrainfall with localized flooding possible. This moisture will \ninteract with the pre-frontal surface trough and/or outflow \nboundaries from the convection expected across the Hill Country. The \nmain cold front is now forecast to move into S TX Friday or Saturday \nwith winds strengthening from the north and cooler temperatures for \nthe weekend. Medium rain chances continue through the weekend as the \nupper low slowly tracks east across TX. \n\nConfidence is medium with respect to increasing rain due to the \nupper low and abundant moisture. However, confidence with respect to \nthe cold front remains low as models have been differing from run to \nrun on timing and just how far south the front goes before stalling. \n\nAs for minor coastal flooding, the P-ETSS indicates tide levels up \nto 1.8 feet above MSL for Tue-Wed during times of high tide. There \nis low confidence that water will reach the dunes due to light \nonshore winds through Tuesday, then 15-25 knots from the south mid-\nweek, swell periods of 7 seconds or less, and the moon phase will be \napproaching the 3rd quarter. None of which are favorable for tidal \noverflow. Will continue to monitor trends for any changes.",
